**Key gcd verifications for the final steps:**
- $217 = 7 \times 31$; $\gcd(26, 217) = 1$ since $26 = 2 \times 13$ shares no factor with $\{7, 31\}$. ✓
- $243 = 3^5$; $\gcd(28, 243) = 1$ since $28 = 2^2 \times 7$ has no factor of 3. ✓
- $271$ is prime (not divisible by 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, and $17^2 = 289 > 271$); $\gcd(29, 271) = 1$. ✓

## Structural observation (consistency check)

Every multiple of 3 (namely 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27) is rejected, because each time we reach $n = 3k$, the running sum $S$ is itself divisible by 3. In fact the sums at those moments are $3, 12, 27, 48, 75, 108, 147, 192, 243 = 3 \times (1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81) = 3k^2$, so $\gcd(3k, 3k^2) = 3k > 1$. This confirms all nine rejections are correct and that no multiple of 3 up to 27 is Secure — exactly accounting for the 9 "No" rows above.

All non-multiples of 3 in the range $[2, 29]$ are accepted, since the running sum $S$ at each such $n$ is a product of primes none of which divide $n$ (verified entry by entry above).

